<p>A standard UNO deck has exactly <strong>108 cards</strong>, and every one of them falls into one of a handful of types. Knowing not just what each card does but <em>how many of them exist</em> is the quiet foundation underneath every good decision at the table — it's the difference between hoping a card shows up and knowing roughly how likely it is. This is a complete card-by-card tour of the deck, with the counts that matter.</p>

<h2>The Four Colors</h2>
<p>UNO's cards come in four colors — <strong>red, yellow, green, and blue</strong> — plus the black wild cards that belong to no color. Each of the four colors has an identical set of cards, so anything true of red is true of blue. That symmetry is worth remembering: when you're counting what's left in a color, you know the starting numbers were the same for all four.</p>

<h2>Number Cards (76 cards)</h2>
<p>The bulk of the deck is number cards, running 0 through 9:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>One "0" per color</strong> — just four zeros in the whole deck.</li>
  <li><strong>Two of each number 1–9 per color</strong> — so eighteen numbered cards per color beyond the zero.</li>
</ul>
<p>That's 19 number cards per color, or 76 in total. The takeaway for play: <strong>zeros are rare</strong> (only four exist), while every other number is comparatively common. If your house plays the Seven-Zero rule where zero rotates all hands, that scarcity is why the card feels special. Number cards carry their face value in points if a round is scored, which is why good players shed high numbers early — more on that in <a href="/blog/how-to-win-at-uno">how to win at UNO</a>.</p>

<h2>Skip (8 cards)</h2>
<p>Two Skips per color. When played, the next player in turn order loses their turn entirely — play jumps over them. Skips are a precision tool: the ideal moment is when the player immediately after you is close to winning. In a two-player game a Skip simply gives you another turn.</p>

<h2>Reverse (8 cards)</h2>
<p>Two Reverses per color. Reverse flips the direction of play — clockwise becomes counter-clockwise and vice versa. It's more strategic than it looks: if the player before you is loading up attacks aimed at you, a Reverse bounces the flow back toward them. In a two-player game, Reverse behaves exactly like a Skip, handing you another turn.</p>

<h2>Draw Two / +2 (8 cards)</h2>
<p>Two Draw Twos per color. The next player must pick up two cards and forfeit their turn — unless you're playing with stacking house rules, in which case they can pile their own +2 on top and pass a growing penalty along. Officially there's no stacking, but it's one of the most popular house rules in the world; see our <a href="/rules/stacking">stacking rules guide</a> for how it actually works.</p>

<h2>Wild (4 cards)</h2>
<p>Four Wild cards, all black. A Wild can be played on top of any card at any time, and the player who plays it chooses which color the game continues in. Because it always matches, a Wild is your escape hatch when nothing in your hand fits the current color. The catch is that a Wild is worth 50 points if you're caught holding it at the end of a scored round, so it's a card to use, not hoard forever.</p>

<h2>Wild Draw Four / +4 (4 cards)</h2>
<p>Four Wild Draw Four cards, the most powerful in the deck. You choose the new color <em>and</em> the next player draws four cards and is skipped. The official rule is that you may only play it when you have no other card matching the current color — and if an opponent suspects you broke that rule, they can challenge you (the "Wild Challenge" house rule builds this in). A well-timed +4 can end a game; spent too early, it's just an expensive color change. Our guide on <a href="/blog/when-to-play-wild-cards">when to play your wild cards</a> covers the timing in depth.</p>

<h2>Putting the Counts Together</h2>
<p>Here's the full deck at a glance:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>76 number cards</strong> — one 0 and two each of 1–9, per color</li>
  <li><strong>8 Skips</strong> — two per color</li>
  <li><strong>8 Reverses</strong> — two per color</li>
  <li><strong>8 Draw Twos</strong> — two per color</li>
  <li><strong>4 Wild cards</strong></li>
  <li><strong>4 Wild Draw Four cards</strong></li>
</ul>
<p>That's 108 cards. Notice that <strong>action and wild cards make up 32 of them</strong> — nearly a third of the deck is designed to disrupt. That's why UNO rarely plays out as a quiet race: there's almost always something in someone's hand ready to change color, skip a turn, or dump cards on you.</p>

<h2>Why the Counts Matter</h2>
<p>You don't need to memorize the deck to use this. Just knowing the rough proportions sharpens your instincts: there are only eight Skips total, so if you've seen several blue Skips already, the blue you're sitting on is safer than it looks. Zeros are almost never in anyone's hand. And with only four of each wild, once a couple have been played, the odds of a surprise +4 landing on you drop. This is the gentle, memory-free version of <a href="/blog/uno-card-counting-and-odds">card counting in UNO</a> — noticing, not memorizing.</p>

<h2>What About Extra Cards?</h2>
<p>Some editions and online modes add cards beyond the classic 108. Our <a href="/uno-no-mercy">No Mercy mode</a>, for example, mixes in Wild +8 cards (draw eight instead of four) and Wild Shuffle Hands cards that scramble everyone's hand. Those are optional extras layered on top of the deck described here — the 108-card core stays the same, which is why understanding it first makes every variant easier to read.</p>

<p>Know the deck, and you're no longer just reacting to whatever you draw — you're playing with a sense of what's still out there. That awareness, more than any single clever play, is what separates a beginner from someone the table has learned to watch.</p>

